  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=1-3x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\,\u27f9\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(1-3x)^{-3} & =3\\int {u}^{-3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\int {u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(-2)}{u}^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-3x)^{-2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2(1-3x)^{2}}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{(1-3x)^{2}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(1-3x)^{-2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2)(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1-3x)+0 \\\\  & =-(1-3x)^{-3}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =3(1-3x)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(1-3x)^{3}}\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$",
